WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.900
SpaceX will buy up to 14 megahertz of paired spectrum in the 800 megahertz range, subject to final regulatory approval. The company plans to combine this with its existing two gigahertz holdings and a terrestrial network to reach devices inside buildings. Elon Musk called the move a very big deal. The announcement follows FCC approval for 15,000 next-generation direct-to-device satellites that deliver more than 100 times the bandwidth of current starlink mobile units. The new frequencies target improved

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:34.460
indoor penetration and wide-area coverage without extra ground towers in every gap.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:40.800
SpaceX intends to integrate the 800 MHz allocation into the V2 satellite payload design.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:48.140
This is SpaceX Daily, Episode 125. Starship Ship 40, the upper stage flown on flight

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:53.420
13, arrived back at Starbase aboard the Vessel Forte after months in the Indian Ocean.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:59.220
Teams removed several heat shield tiles for reuse on flight 14 and conducted in-water

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:04.280
inspections near Christmas Island. The intact recovery gave engineers physical data on

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:09.560
tile performance that cameras alone could not provide. The landing burn on Flight 13 was

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.340
performed in a profile that matches the final descent needed for a future tower catch attempt.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.220
SpaceX is planning a dedicated heavy haul corridor to move oversized starship hardware and ground

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:25.420
support equipment to the Texas launch site. The route would reduce reliance on public roads and

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.060
waterways for oversized loads.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.860
Dedicated infrastructure would allow larger single-piece components to reach the production

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.600
and launch areas without repeated disassembly.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.720
The company filed details for a large dwarf and internal road network to support starship

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.940
component transport and operations along the Louisiana coast.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.940
The dwarf would accommodate heavy lift vessels delivering booster and ship sections directly

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:48.640
to the site.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.280
Internal roads would connect the waterfront to assembly buildings without crossing public

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.000
infrastructure.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.440
American Airlines will install Starlink terminals across its Airbus narrow-body fleet after

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.180
initial testing on select routes.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:06.100
The move follows earlier narrow-body trials and aims to provide high-speed connectivity

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.340
on domestic flights.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:12.780
American Airlines plans to equip the entire narrow-body fleet rather than limiting service

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:13.540
to wide-body.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:18.940
The phased rollout will begin with aircraft already operating on high-demand domestic segments.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:23.480
components. Terminal installation will use the same hardware validated in prior Starlink

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.700
aviation tests. Multiple recent conjunctions brought Starlink satellites within tens to

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:33.400
hundreds of meters of other objects. The company stated the distances were way too close

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:38.520
to comfort and called for better data sharing among operators. SpaceX highlighted that

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:44.080
current coordination processes left insufficient margin during several events. The firm requested

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:47.720
standardized ephemeralist exchange formats to reduce response times.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.900
Improved protocols would apply across both commercial and government satellite fleets operating

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:53.980
in low-earth orbit.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:58.980
The platform is developing a single plan that combines access to X premium features,

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:01.880
GROC models, and the cursor code editor.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.020
The bundle would streamline billing for users who rely on both the social platform and AI

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:07.960
development tools.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.660
Subscribers would gain unified access without separate logins or payment methods.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:17.680
The offering targets developers who already use GROC within cursor for code assistance.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.900
X expects the combined tier to increase retention across its AI and social products.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:28.280
Ismail Elmos, who led cursor operations across Europe while previously working at SpaceX,

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:30.020
is joining OpenAI.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.440
The move highlights competition for talent between AI coding tools and frontier model companies.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:40.740
Elmos had overseen European expansion of the cursor editor that integrates multiple large

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:41.600
language models.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.980
His departure follows a period of rapid growth for Anisphere, the company behind Cursor.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:53.120
Open AI gains an executive familiar with both SpaceX engineering culture and AI developer

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:53.560
tooling.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:59.180
Worth keeping an eye on the FCC plays certain spectrum requests on hold and impose restrictions

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:02.380
on frequencies awarded in the recent Starlink mobile approval.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:08.500
SpaceX must still secure final approval for the 800 MHz transaction and demonstrate that

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.340
the combined satellite terrestrial network meets interference and coverage rules.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:17.020
Those steps will determine how quickly the service can scale beyond initial test areas.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:22.820
The engineering reality is that low-band spectrum at 800 MHz travels farther through obstacles

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:24.980
than higher frequencies already in use.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:30.160
Pairing the new allocation with existing holdings allows one satellite beam to serve indoor

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.520
handsets, while a ground layer handles dense urban spots.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:39.000
slots. The planned 100 times bandwidth jump on V2 satellites then multiplies capacity

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.660
once the signal arrives at the phone. This set up trades some spectrum efficiency for

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:48.780
simpler user devices that need no special terminal. The same orbital hardware can therefore

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.720
support both consumer broadband and mobile backhaul without added mass or power beyond

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:59.600
current Starship margins. Ship 40 returned to starbase after its flight 13 splashdown in

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:04.380
Indian Ocean. Recovery teams towed the intact upper stage through the Port of Brown's

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:09.780
ville Channel, despite earlier concerns the operation might fail. Engineers removed several

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:15.260
heat shield tiles from the vehicle and flew them again on Flight 14, providing direct physical

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:20.300
evidence of tile durability after reentry heating. Because the stage remained intact rather

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:25.480
than exploding on contact with the water, space X obtained data on tile attachment and thermal

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:29.040
protection that camera equipped satellites alone could not supply.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:33.920
This physical inspection shortens the iteration loop for the heat shield redesign required

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.920
before repeated booster and ship reuse becomes routine.

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:41.520
Starship upper stage recovery changes the economics of the entire stack.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:46.500
When a ship survives splash down, the cost per flight drops from the raw material price

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:51.660
of a new vehicle to the marginal cost of inspection, tile replacement, and refurbishment.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.840
The gap between those two numbers is the idiot index for the upper stage.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:00.840
Flight 13 demonstrated that the current tile attachment method can survive one reentry

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.440
without total loss, lowering that index.

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:08.980
The next engineering step is to close the remaining distance to zero refurbishment time so that

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.980
the same ship can fly again within days rather than months.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:17.280
Achieving that target requires both the tower catch hardware now under construction at starbase,

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.440
and the orbital refueling demonstration still ahead in the flight test campaign.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.000
A quick look at the tape shows SPCX closed the session roughly flat.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:33.220
Before we go, SpaceX targets October 15th for the Falcon 9 launch of the USSF 481 mission

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:36.000
from Vandenberg using a flight-proven booster.

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:39.260
That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today.

